  "title": "The next digit",
  "body": " The next digit  One third fits on a scrap of paper. Its decimal expansion would use up all the paper in the world and still be asking for more. The fraction is finished as soon as we lift the pencil; the instruction to write its digits has scarcely begun. We resolve this inconvenience with three dots: Three marks excuse us from making infinitely many more. It is an excellent arrangement, provided we know what we have been excused from doing.  Here we do. Another three belongs after the last one we wrote. Long division supplies it, along with a remainder that asks us to do exactly the same thing again. Somewhere in childhood we became capable of setting this work in motion. We were generally asked to stop after a few decimal places, which was considerate of the teacher. The calculation itself offers no such concession.  A decimal beginning, however long, can promise less than it seems. After a hundred threes a four might appear. It would no longer be one third, but it would be a perfectly respectable decimal. The marks already on the paper cannot object. Our certainty about the next three comes from the division we are doing, from the reason for the marks. This small distinction—between what has been written and what governs its continuation—will keep widening.  We will begin at the desk, with a human being employed to calculate. Following the pencil through an ordinary division will reveal how much a few instructions can accomplish. We will use instructions to write a decimal that escapes every fraction, and then share the pencil with an opponent who would prefer a different outcome. The game opens onto René Baire’s mathematics: there is room inside every finite beginning for more than either player can see.  Alonzo Church and three of his students, Alan Turing, Stephen Kleene, and Leon Henkin, will enter as our demands grow. We want digits, then assurances that the digits will arrive, then proofs of those assurances. Directions that can be followed without understanding turn out to be capable of making understanding very uncomfortable. A proof about a calculation can itself become part of the calculation, with consequences for the proof.  Eventually we will return to long division with two programs and a surprise. Keep the programs fixed; choose the decimal you would like their quotient to follow. Even a decimal beyond the reach of ordinary computation can be approached in this way, as far along its digits as we can ask—in a suitable mathematical world. That last qualification will take some earning. An answer already reached by an ordinary calculation stays put. What changes is our account of a calculation that we can never finish watching.  For now there is a pencil, a sheet, and a division to be done. The infinite part can wait. It is rather good at that.  "
