  "title": "The small alphabet",
  "body": " The small alphabet  Besides membership and equality, the primitive logical signs are and the contradiction marker . Variable names can be written as v followed by an ordinary numeral, as in v0 , v1 , v17 . The finitely many digit marks therefore provide as many distinct names as a proof needs. A term is an expression naming an object. In our basic language it is just a variable, or a constant once constants have been added. A variable leaves its object available for assignment; an added constant is a name whose assigned object is kept fixed within a model.  Our two set axioms are relational. They assert that suitable objects exist; they do not identify a unique empty object or a unique result of adjunction. The notation for an empty object and for an adjoined object means that witnesses have been chosen. For any particular finite construction, this notation can be expanded into successive existence statements and fresh witness branches. For example, tells us to use adjunction to choose an object whose members are those of the chosen empty object together with . Its members are consequently exactly . The notation records that choice, not a uniqueness claim. We use it only as this abbreviation for the successive witness choices.  The starting formulas are , , and . The first two are called atomic : they have no smaller statements joined inside them. The contradiction marker records failure. Parentheses and the logical signs build larger formulas. A formula is any grammatically permitted statement pattern. A sentence has no free variables left awaiting assignment. For example, contains free variables, whereas is a sentence. Being a sentence says nothing about being true. We use the abbreviations The two AS axioms are the closed sentences Empty and Adjunction from Chapter  , expanded by these conventions. The abbreviation expansion is mechanical.  Each node of a proof tree records a formula, the rule used there, its premise nodes, and any substitution or discharge labels. Each occurrence of an assumption gets its own label. This is how the checker follows what a line depends on: it brings the premises’ open assumptions forward, removing only the labeled occurrences the rule explicitly discharges. A line in an unrelated closed branch remains unavailable, however convenient its conclusion would be. A theorem has no open assumptions except the designated axioms.  For example, a conjunction node may record premises and , conclusion , and rule keep both. The checker matches each conjunct to its corresponding premise and carries both premises’ open-assumption lists into this node. Replacing only the second conjunct by makes the node fail. No discharge occurs at this card: even a correctly matched conjunction still depends on the assumptions used to obtain its parts.  "
