  "body": " The primitive cards  Keep both; take either.  The open assumptions of a combined derivation are the union of those needed by its premises.  Find a contradiction; reject a supposition.  The second card discharges precisely the assumption occurrences labeled . Other assumptions remain open.  Classical reductio.  Discharge the designated occurrences. From an already established contradiction one may derive any by opening an unused assumption , repeating the contradiction, and applying reductio. Double-negation elimination is also derived: assume , contradict , and conclude .  Give an example.  The notation means replacing the free occurrences of by the term . The substitution must not cause any variable of to become bound. For example, if is and is , then is . The slash means put the expression on the left in the free places named on the right. It is not division. If is , replacing free by first requires renaming the bound letter. Write , then obtain . The new free name still names the bag, rather than becoming the unknown member sought by the existential sign.  Use an unknown witness.  The temporary variable is absent from , from , and from every undischarged assumption other than the designated copies of . Discharge those copies. Rename bound variables beforehand if necessary to make the temporary name entirely fresh. A fresh-constant version gives the same rule if the language extension is recorded.  Equality.  Here is a displayed template; both substitutions must be capture-free. Its chosen free occurrences of specify which occurrences are being replaced. Bound occurrences retain their original scope.  For every substitution, the checker parses the enclosing quantifiers. It may first rename all bound variables to fresh indexes larger than every index in the formula and inserted term. It then replaces only the designated free occurrences. These finite scans check the scope conditions.  "
