  "title": "Assembled cards",
  "body": " Assembled cards  The primitive cards can be joined into the familiar moves below. For example, if gathers a short argument under an assumption and then closes that assumption. The expansions show where each temporary assumption goes, so a proof written with the convenient larger cards still uses exactly the same calculus.  If: introduction. Suppose a derivation of has additional labeled assumptions . Assume ; take , feed it into the given derivation, and take from the conjunction. The resulting contradiction gives . The assumptions used in the supplied derivation have been replaced by consequences of the newly discharged conjunction. Other assumptions survive.  If: use. From and , assume , join it to , and derive a contradiction. Reductio gives .  Or: give either alternative. From , assume , extract , and contradict . Discharge the conjunction to obtain the abbreviation . The other side is symmetric.  Cases. Suppose , a derivation of from additional assumptions , and a derivation of the same from additional assumptions . Assume . Inside that branch, assume , use the first derivation, and derive ; discharge to obtain . Repeat with to obtain . Their conjunction contradicts . Reductio discharges and yields . Precisely the designated case assumptions are discharged; all other assumptions in both case derivations remain open.  Every: use a particular object. Given , assume . Give as an example for , derive contradiction, and conclude by reductio. Substitution remains capture-free.  Every: prove an arbitrary instance. Suppose has been derived, where is not free in any open assumption and is free for the indicated substitution. Choose a fresh variable and rename the arbitrary parameter throughout that derivation to obtain . Now assume . Use a fresh witness branch , derive contradiction with , and eliminate the witness to obtain . Discharging the existential assumption proves . Bound variables may be renamed beforehand so that is absent everywhere required by the witness card. This is the usual universal-introduction rule with its exact open-assumption restriction.  Soundness follows by checking that each primitive card preserves truth under its open assumptions; reductio uses classical truth values. The assembled rules therefore preserve truth as well. The Henkin construction uses the derived implication and universal rules just established.  "
