  "level": "1",
  "url": "appendix-proof-checking-and-enumerating.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "B.4",
  "title": "Checking and enumerating",
  "body": " Checking and enumerating  To put a proof tree on a row of paper, use balanced delimiters to mark its branches and record each formula, rule label, premise subtree, and discharge instruction. The checker first parses the tree, then works from leaves to root. At each node it checks the rule pattern and substitution and calculates the assumptions still open. A forbidden witness name or an unsupported axiom ends the attempt. The requested conclusion is accepted only when every check has passed. All the scans are through the finite record supplied, so the checking finishes.  The search feeds this checker every finite string of the proof alphabet, ordered first by length and then alphabetically. It keeps the successful proofs. An endless mechanically generated list of additional axioms is allowed: an axiom leaf must then bring a finite record showing its axiom being produced by that list’s enumerator. The checker examines the attached record. It never has to wait to see whether a missing axiom will someday appear. Each certificate is therefore decidably checkable, and every valid certificate eventually has its turn in the search.  "
